              <h3>What are different languages used for</h3>
<p>Please see the following video on coding language and programming terms.</p>

              <!-- sections.njk VIDEO! -->
              
              
              
      <div class="l-video-container">
        <video poster="~/images/svg/poster.svg" controls preload="metadata" aria-labelledby="watch" width="1000"
          data-base="uc_u1_s1_p4_programming_terms" 
          data-ext=".mp4" 
          data-multi="false"
          data-trans="uc_u1_s1_p4_programming_terms undefined">
          <source src="https://kpcontent.blob.core.windows.net/$web/resources/v2l/720/uc_u1_s1_p4_programming_terms.mp4" type="video/mp4">
          <track default label="English" kind="captions" srclang="en" src="~/videos/captions/vtt/uc_u1_s1_p4_programming_terms.vtt">
          <p>Sorry, your browser doesn't support embedded videos</p>
        </video>
      </div>
      
          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Leah:</strong> We can see by looking at the different languages that can be learnt that programming languages can be used for the various functions that computers and devices can perform.</p>
<p>Let's look at some of the terms that are used in relation to different types of language and programming.</p>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> Programming Paradigm</p>
<p>A programming paradigm doesn't refer to a specific language but rather to a way to program. Some languages make it easy to write in some paradigms, but not others.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Functional Programming</p>
<p>Functional programming languages are based on mathematical functions that use linear mathematical logic. They work well with list processing applications and large data analysis.</p>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> Object Oriented</p>
<p>The most popular programming languages in the current arena as we have seen, are known as 'object oriented'. This means that the language is organised around groupings of data also called 'objects'. These types of languages are therefore often easy to manipulate, reuse and scale, making them quite versatile and ideal for structuring software.</p>
<p>So, let's look at the differences between object oriented languages and procedural programming and the reasons most popular modern languages have adopted the Object Oriented style.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Procedural programming</p>
<p>In procedural programming, a program is divided into small parts also known as 'functions' [routines and sub routines]. This is often referred to as inline programming taking a form of top-down approach, solving problems from the top of the code to the bottom, through a series of steps that must be carried out.</p>
<p>These procedures are broken down through 'functional decomposition' until a sub procedure is simple enough to be solved. If, however, edits are needed, then every line of code associated to the original change needs to be edited too. This makes it harder to locate all the related elements that need to be altered. Procedural programming does not also, have a proper method for hiding data so it is less secure. Adding data and function is not easy and the function is often more important than the data itself.</p>
<p>Examples of languages used in procedural programming are FORTRAN, ALGOL, COBOL, BASIC, Pascal and C.</p>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> Object Oriented Programming</p>
<p>Object Oriented Programming is a programming model based around the principle of objects. It uses collections of objects to pass on messages when called upon, to request specific services or information. Objects contain data in the form of attributes and code.</p>
<p>Object oriented programming has access specifiers such as private, public, protected and this ability to hide data means it is more secure. Adding data is easier as new objects can inherit characteristics from existing ones making adjustment of programs simpler. Data is more important than the function and it is based more on the real-world environment. This form of programming is a bottom-up approach. Many object-oriented programming languages will dump or destroy unused objects, freeing up system memory which enables systems to run faster and more effectively.</p>
<p>Examples of languages used in object-oriented programming are Java, JavaScript, C++, C#, Python, PHP, Ruby, etc. As you can see by the list of languages, we have looked at already they all fall into the category of object oriented languages.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
